#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hypeForge's look, applied: one command writes every program's look from a STYLE (shapes,
layout) and a THEME (colours), sets the theme's wallpaper, and can go back (D-78, step 1).

    hypeforge-theme list                      the styles and the themes
    hypeforge-theme current                   what is applied now
    hypeforge-theme apply <style> <theme>     write every look file, then reload what's running
    hypeforge-theme wallpaper theme|<file>    follow the theme's picture, or keep one of yours
    hypeforge-theme undo                      put back the files from before the last apply

Options for apply: --dry-run (print what would change, write nothing), --no-reload,
--root DIR (write under DIR instead of ~/.config: the hidden bench uses this).

What it writes (the configs include or import these, so a switch rewrites only them):
  ~/.config/hypeforge/theme/sway.conf          window colours, borders, gaps, pointer, wallpaper
  ~/.config/hypeforge/theme/colors.css          the bar's and the lock screen's colours
  ~/.config/hypeforge/theme/fuzzel-colors.ini   the pop-up lists
  ~/.config/mako/config                          notifications (whole file, from its template)
  ~/.config/gtk-3.0|gtk-4.0/gtk.css, settings.ini  GTK apps (also undoes what a KDE login wrote, F-52)
  ~/.config/gtklock/background.jpg               the lock screen's blurred copy of the wallpaper
Every apply keeps a backup of what it replaces (the last 20).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

